86045417] [17592186502065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501751 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502065 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186501826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502065 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502065 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502065 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186501826 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502065 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fe9a2f0e-9ecf-5c31-b505-d6c46aeae913" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502086 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502014 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502086 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142574" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502086 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502086 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=16$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $5.4$ and the sample standard deviation is $8,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.947)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $15$ degrees of freedom.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502086 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "27ec3d52-24b4-5797-862b-64e2b932e1b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502086 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}-{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}}\\le \\mu \\le \\overset{―}{x}+{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=5.4,\\,n=16,\\,s=8.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.947)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{15,0.005}=2.947.\n$$\n\n![](images/bb/bbaa00deb18e38f1b201317615a790764519ad5d2c5b436fe13044a09c71beac.png)\n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5.4 & \\pm 2.947\\cdot \\frac{8}{\\sqrt{16}} \\\\ 5.4 & \\pm 5.894\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502087 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502021 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502087 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142518" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502087 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502087 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=81$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $12,$ and the sample standard deviation is $5,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.664)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $80$ degrees of freedom.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502087 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2d606c2e-8088-532b-9f1f-331fecee3381" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502087 #edb/ref 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=81\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*.  \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=12,\\,n=81,\\,s=5.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.664)=0.05.$ As a result,  \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{80,0.05}=1.664.\n$$\n\n![](images/c1/c1bf3a9f49bb4b4fb24d4fb39bdf0b02743efd1c280875332e5f3bbc1539d953.png)\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & \\pm 1.664\\cdot \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{81}} \\\\ 12 & \\pm 0.924\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502095 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502095 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143026" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502095 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502095 #edb/ref 1071 "A thread manufacturer tests the tensile strength of a sample containing $9$ pieces of a particular type of thread.\nThe mean tensile strength of the sample is $7.8\\,\\text{lb}$ with a standard deviation of $0.06\\,\\text{lb}.$\nAssuming that tensile strengths are normally distributed, find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean tensile strength  $\\mu $ of this thread. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $\\,1.860\\,$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $\\,3.355\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502095 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c887832-1efe-5629-bd9f-7e1600f20497" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502095 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=7.8\\,\\text{lb},\\,n=9,\\,s=0.06\\,\\text{lb}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{8,0.005}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{8,0.005})=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=9-1=8$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/11/1199336fffafda6d04b4e9562aec6b1bbc1726005a54180c8e8ed4935981ddd1.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{8,0.005}=3.355:$  \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $\\,1.860\\,$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $3.355$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |\n\n   \n\nTherefore, a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7.8 & \\pm 3.355\\cdot \\frac{0.06}{\\sqrt{9}}\\,\\text{lb} \\\\ 7.8 & \\pm 0.067\\,\\text{lb}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502102 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502102 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502102 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502102 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=225$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $98,$ and the sample standard deviation is $30,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.598)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $224$ degrees of freedom.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502102 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ae17796-447a-5d04-9973-717e1d847688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502102 #edb/ref 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=225\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*.\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=98,\\,n=225,\\,s=30.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.598)=0.005.$ As a result,\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{224,0.005}=2.598.\n$$\n\n![](images/2b/2b711e6fe35f64d886472fa38c5a65afd7473b344949d7effd49405946f3cee5.png)\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}98 & \\pm 2.598\\cdot \\frac{30}{\\sqrt{225}} \\\\ 98 & \\pm 5.196\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502103 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502103 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142701" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502103 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502103 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=16$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $45$ and the sample standard deviation is $9,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.947)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $15$ degrees of freedom.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502103 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4c5ba2c5-2aab-5530-84f9-62121bb3609f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502103 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-dis