186502118 #edb/ref 1071 "A random sample of $100$ students from a particular university yields a mean GPA of $3.1$ with a sample standard deviation of $0.48.$ Find a  $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean GPA $\\mu $ of all students.  \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $99$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $100$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $101$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.625\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502118 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7a68a1f9-858f-5d69-bc49-809a211decce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502118 #edb/ref 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=100\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=3.1,\\,n=100,\\,s=0.48.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{99,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{99,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=100-1=99$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/1b/1bb4e66b68bbc1fb55a36abafb4f184fb6b25b16314e989a47fe6b3a835cea65.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{99,0.05}=1.660:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $99$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $1.660$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $100$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.626\\,$ |\n| $101$ | $\\,1.290\\,$ | $\\,1.660\\,$ | $\\,1.984\\,$ | $\\,2.364\\,$ | $\\,2.625\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.1 & \\pm 1.66\\cdot \\frac{0.48}{\\sqrt{100}}\\,\\text{grade points} \\\\ 3.1 & \\pm 0.08\\text{ grade points}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502134 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502134 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142571" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502134 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502134 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4c/4c9b1e09734e74c098fd5e91eb3c72079157c3a69b1bb1d039bb46e823e5b7b6.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=25$ from a normal population. Given that the sample mean is $35.5,$ and the sample standard deviation is $3,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.064)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $24$ degrees of freedom,  as shown in the diagram above.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502134 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aecd358b-06cc-5c20-9edb-c893e2b56662" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502134 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom. \n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=35.5,\\,n=25,\\,s=3.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.064)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{24,0.025}=2.064.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}35.5 & \\pm 2.064\\cdot \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{25}} \\\\ 35.5 & \\pm 1.238\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502135 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502011 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502135 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142515" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502135 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502135 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider a sample of size $n=400$ from a population. Given that the sample mean is $57,$ and the sample standard deviation is $26,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.649)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $399$ degrees of freedom.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502135 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b0647362-6c93-5a80-87bb-0e4349d8ef9c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502135 #edb/ref 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=400\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case, \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=57,\\,n=400,\\,s=26.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.649)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}={t}_{399,0.05}=1.649.\n$$\n\n![](images/7d/7d2cc71801ad3826dddfb670518ad038ccbf11f363569896fe3c3b673e5c58a6.png)\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}57 & \\pm 1.649\\cdot \\frac{26}{\\sqrt{400}} \\\\ 57 & \\pm 2.144\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502139 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186502018 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502139 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143033" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502139 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502139 #edb/ref 1071 "A sample of the different prices of a given product in $64$ randomly selected stores in a given state yields a mean price of $$37.15$ with a sample standard deviation of $$2.00.$  Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean price of the product  $\\mu $ across the state. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $62$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.670\\,$ | $\\,1.999\\,$ | $\\,2.388\\,$ | $\\,2.657\\,$ |\n| $63$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.387\\,$ | $\\,2.656\\,$ |\n| $64$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.386\\,$ | $\\,2.655\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502139 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b9cb4d01-534f-524c-85ee-ddefd795826f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502139 #edb/ref 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=64\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=$37.15,\\,n=64,\\,s=$2.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.90=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{99,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{63,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=64-1=63$ degrees of freedom. \n\n![](images/43/43ddb73bfc30887b0de7b7debf98407dbcea330ebe55c485982d5c05c0681c9a.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{63,0.05}=1.669:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $62$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.670\\,$ | $\\,1.999\\,$ | $\\,2.388\\,$ | $\\,2.657\\,$ |\n| $63$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $1.669$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.387\\,$ | $\\,2.656\\,$ |\n| $64$ | $\\,1.295\\,$ | $\\,1.669\\,$ | $\\,1.998\\,$ | $\\,2.386\\,$ | $\\,2.655\\,$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, the $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}37.15 & \\pm 1.669\\cdot \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{64}}\\text{ dollars} \\\\ 37.15 & \\pm 0.417\\text{ dollars}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502149 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186501979 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502149 #edb/ref 1069 "q-142921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502149 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502149 #edb/ref 1071 "The body mass index (BMI) of Dr. Lee's patients is normally distributed. A random sample of $9$ patients yields a mean BMI of $25.5\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}$ with a standard deviation of $2.6\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}.$ Find a $90%$ confidence interval for the population mean BMI $\\mu $ among all of Dr. Lee's patients. \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $\\,1.860\\,$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $\\,3.355\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502149 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d3b129d2-0cd5-58e9-b5e8-9079b387fb53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502149 #edb/ref 1074 "We are told that the population is normally distributed, but we do not know the population variance $\\sigma .$\n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=25.5\\,\\,n=9,\\,s=2.6\\,.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{8,0.05}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{8,0.05})=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=9-1=8$ degrees of freedom. \n\n![](images/ce/ce7d5fc9bbf2c9e28a0862247e6f762cad8a718401828070e96041e7f82b1891.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{8,0.05}=1.860:$   \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $7$ | $\\,1.415\\,$ | $\\,1.895\\,$ | $\\,2.365\\,$ | $\\,2.998\\,$ | $\\,3.499\\,$ |\n| $8$ | $\\,1.397\\,$ | $1.860$ | $\\,2.306\\,$ | $\\,2.896\\,$ | $\\,3.355\\,$ |\n| $9$ | $\\,1.383\\,$ | $\\,1.833\\,$ | $\\,2.262\\,$ | $\\,2.821\\,$ | $\\,3.250\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 25.5\\pm 1.860\\cdot \\frac{2.6}{\\sqrt{9}}\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2} \\\\  & 25.5\\pm 1.612\\,\\text{kg}/{\\text{m}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
